# Slurm matrix builder

One TOML file expands into multiple jobs, config files, branches, and
hyperparameter combinations. `plan` and `render` work without Slurm; only
`submit` needs `sbatch`. Choose the `action` in the dashboard or as the first
positional argument.

The generated scripts keep the useful safety checks from the former Bunya
submission script: account and GPU defaults, clean Conda activation, scratch
caches, GPU checks, one-time locked preparation, and a branch guard. Jobs
verify the requested branch but never run `git checkout`, so parallel
submissions cannot race over one worktree.

## TOML layout

```toml
[cluster]
account = "a_research"
partition = "gpu_cuda"
qos = "gpu"
gres = "gpu:a100:1"
constraint = "cuda80gb"
cpus_per_task = 4
mem = "32G"
time = "08:00:00"

[environment]
modules = ["miniforge/26.1.0-0"]
conda_env = "/scratch/user/alice/conda/envs/flowIDS"
scratch_base = "/scratch/user/{user}"
check_gpu = true
launcher = ["srun", "--ntasks=1"]

[environment.variables]
TOKENIZERS_PARALLELISM = "false"

[[jobs]]
name = "flowids"
branch = "m0"
workdir = "../flowIDS"
configs = ["configs/*.toml"]
prepare = ["python", "scripts/get_dataset.py"]
prepare_once = true
command = [
  "python", "-m", "src.main",
  "--config", "{config}",
  "--seed", "{seed}",
  "--learning-rate", "{learning_rate}"
]

[jobs.matrix]
seed = [1, 2, 3]
learning_rate = [0.001, 0.0001]
```

Each config is combined with every matrix value. Placeholders supported in
`command` and `prepare` are `{config}`, `{branch}`, `{workdir}`, and every
matrix key. Add more `[[jobs]]` tables for unrelated commands or branches.
The default `--limit 1000` catches accidental explosions before submission.
With `prepare_once = true`, a lock and success marker derived from the prepare
command let only the first job prepare shared input. Remove the matching
`.slurm-prepare-*.done` marker from the work directory to prepare it again.

If `[cluster].account` is omitted, an `a_*` Unix group is used. Bunya defaults
select `gpu_cuda`, one A100, `gpu`, and `cuda80gb`; explicitly set cluster
values for other sites or CPU jobs. `environment.conda_env` must be absolute.
Rendered files are private to the current user (`0700`). Do not put secrets in
the TOML: environment variables are written into the generated batch script.
Existing output files are never overwritten.

## Direct CLI

```sh
slurm plan --config docs/examples/slurm.toml
slurm plan --config slurm.toml --json
slurm render --config slurm.toml --output slurm-build
slurm render --config slurm.toml --output slurm-build --apply
slurm submit --config slurm.toml --apply
```

`render` and `submit` preview the exact files first. Direct applies require
typing `APPLY`; the shared TUI confirms with `CLEAN` and then supplies
`--apply --yes`.
